FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2044 — module-value recording is gated on `top_module` from `frames.last().and_then(|frame| frame.module)` (`src/lsp.rs:1930`). Inside an `if`/`loop` in a module body the top frame is the block frame (module `None`), so a replacing `set` is never added to `module_values`, even though the same walk has already deleted the same-named published function (`src/lsp.rs:1944-1953`, which deliberately walks to the innermost runtime frame because the write reaches the module scope) and the new binding is placed in the module's scope by `program_position = test_scopes.last().max(module_scopes.last())` (`src/lsp.rs:2009-2013`). Concretely, `module M / to f() give back 1 end / if yes then set f to 2 end / export all / end / say M.f` publishes `M.f` at runtime (`interpreter.rs:1544-1549` reads the module's local scopes, and `export all` still sees the top-level `to f`), but `goto_definition`/`find_references` on `M.f` answer `None`/empty. VERIFIED: traced the read lines and the runtime `declare_member`/`set_var` (interpreter.rs:1544-1549, 1881-1889); no test covers the nested-block replacement. Fix: derive the owning module for `module_values` from the innermost runtime frame, exactly as the function-removal code at 1944-1953 does, and add a fixture.

2. [MAJOR] src/lsp.rs:2014-2015 — the `constant` branch sets `program_position` from `test_scopes` only (ignoring `module_scopes`), so a `constant` written inside a `module` body is bound in the file's program scope; nevertheless line 2044 records it in `module_values` as a published member, and the new test `edge_goto_definition_on_a_declared_module_value_resolves_to_its_declaration` (`tests/tooling_grammar_test.rs:5218-5225`) asserts `Counter.label` resolves. The runtime reads a module member only out of the module's local scopes (`interpreter.rs:1544-1549`), and this file's own comment (`src/lsp.rs:1962-1964`) and the REPORT state the VM binds a constant to a global regardless of block. If that is accurate, the runtime never publishes `Counter.label`, so the test pins navigation to a phantom member. VERIFIED only as a trace of the LSP model; settle by running `module M; constant label to "n"; export all; end; say M.label` through `rb`. If it errors, stop recording module `constant`s as published values (or bind module constants in the module scope so navigation and runtime agree); if it succeeds, add a runtime/golden test proving the member exists.

